/dts-v1/;
/plugin/;

/*
 * light-desk: activate internal pull-up input mode for 8 button GPIO
 * pins on PocketBeagle 1 - the 6 control-desk buttons (P2.02, P2.04,
 * P2.06, P2.20, P2.22, P2.24), P2.33 (the stand-alone-mode toggle
 * button, see scripts/standalone_mode_toggle.py and memo.md item 15),
 * and P2.19 (the rotary pulse encoder's own push button - the
 * encoder's A/B quadrature channels are a separate concern, handled
 * by BB-ROTARY-ENCODER-light-desk-00A0.dts's eQEP0 hardware decoder,
 * not GPIO) - read from userspace via libgpiod by
 * scripts/buttons_debug_print.py / scripts/ads7830_to_osc.py /
 * scripts/standalone_mode_toggle.py (see pb1/memo.md item 9 for the
 * original 6-pin selection history).
 *
 * The base am335x-pocketbeagle.dts (see /opt/source/dtb-*.x/src/arm/
 * ti/omap/am335x-pocketbeagle.dts on the board, under &am33xx_pinmux)
 * already defines a ready-made pinctrl-single node per header pin,
 * pre-configured exactly as PIN_INPUT_PULLUP + MUX_MODE7 (GPIO) -
 * P2_02_gpio, P2_04_gpio, P2_06_gpio, P2_20_gpio, P2_22_gpio,
 * P2_24_gpio, P2_33_gpio, P2_19_gpio below - but none of them are
 * referenced by any device's pinctrl-0, so they're never actually
 * applied; the pins sit at whatever mux/bias U-Boot/ROM left them in
 * at boot instead. Confirmed on real hardware: with nothing wired to
 * any of these pins, `gpioget -b pull-up|pull-down|disabled` all
 * returned the exact same fixed value per pin no matter the bias
 * flag - i.e. libgpiod's runtime bias request has zero effect here
 * (this reproduced on unrelated pins too, e.g. P2.02/P2.03, so it's
 * not specific to these - AM335x's pinctrl-single driver doesn't
 * support runtime bias changes through the generic Linux pinctrl API
 * that libgpiod's -b option goes through; the pull config has to be
 * the static pinmux value applied at boot, which is exactly what
 * this overlay does).
 *
 * 6 of the 8 pins (P2.02, P2.04, P2.06, P2.22, P2.24, P2.33) sit on
 * GPIO bank gpio1; P2.20 sits on gpio2; P2.19 sits on gpio0. Three
 * small fragments, one per bank, cover this: any platform device
 * with a bound driver gets pinctrl-0 applied automatically at probe
 * time (generic Linux driver-core behavior, independent of what the
 * driver itself does), and gpio0's/gpio1's/gpio2's own omap-gpio
 * driver instances already probe/bind normally - so adding
 * pinctrl-names/pinctrl-0 here applies the pad config without any
 * device claiming the GPIO lines themselves (unlike e.g. a gpio-keys
 * or gpio-leds node, which would also reserve the lines and block
 * libgpiod from requesting them from userspace - not what we want
 * here, since the existing Python scripts poll these lines directly).
 */

&gpio0 {
	pinctrl-names = "default";
	pinctrl-0 = <&P2_19_gpio>;
};

&gpio1 {
	pinctrl-names = "default";
	pinctrl-0 = <&P2_02_gpio &P2_04_gpio &P2_06_gpio &P2_22_gpio &P2_24_gpio &P2_33_gpio>;
};

&gpio2 {
	pinctrl-names = "default";
	pinctrl-0 = <&P2_20_gpio>;
};
